<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-logarithm-as-integral">
  <title>Properties of the Logarithm Defined by an Integral</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-logarithm-as-integral">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Explain why <m>\ln(1)=0</m> and why the domain of <m>\displaystyle\ln(x)=\int_1^x\frac{1}{t}\,dt</m> is <m>(0,\infty)</m>, directly from the definition.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognize that <m>\displaystyle\int_a^{ab}\frac{1}{t}\,dt=\int_1^b\frac{1}{t}\,dt</m> by comparing Riemann sums. Use this to justify <m>\ln(ab)=\ln(a)+\ln(b)</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the product rule for <m>\ln</m> to obtain the reciprocal rule, the quotient rule, and the power rule <m>\ln(a^p)=p\ln(a)</m> for integers <m>p</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the algebraic properties of <m>\ln</m> to find the logarithm of a product, quotient, or power from known values of <m>\ln(a)</m> and <m>\ln(b)</m>.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Last time we used the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to create a new function that
      no power rule could produce. Today we find out what it can do. Everything we learn about
      this function will come from its definition as an integral, not from any formula or from
      anything you may remember about logarithms.
    </p>
    <p>
      Team Roles: Manager, Recorder, Spokesperson, and Reflector.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <definition xml:id="def-natural-log-integral">
    <title>The Natural Logarithm</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For <m>x\gt 0</m>, the <term>natural logarithm</term> is defined by
        <md>\ln(x) = \int_1^x \frac{1}{t}\,dt\text{.}</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-ln-two-facts">
    <title>Two Facts Straight from the Definition</title>
    <p>
      First, <m>\ln(1)=\displaystyle\int_1^1\frac{1}{t}\,dt=0</m>, since an integral over an
      interval of zero width is zero. Second, the integrand <m>1/t</m> is not defined at
      <m>t=0</m>, and an integral starting at <m>t=1</m> cannot pass through that discontinuity.
      So the domain of <m>\ln(x)</m> is <m>(0,\infty)</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-ln-algebraic-properties">
    <title>Algebraic Properties of <m>\ln</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For all <m>a\gt 0</m> and <m>b\gt 0</m>:
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><em>Product rule:</em> <m>\ln(ab)=\ln(a)+\ln(b)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><em>Reciprocal rule:</em> <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{1}{b}\right)=-\ln(b)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><em>Quotient rule:</em> <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{a}{b}\right)=\ln(a)-\ln(b)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p><em>Power rule:</em> <m>\ln(a^p)=p\,\ln(a)</m> for every real number <m>p</m>.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-ln-deriving-properties" workspace="0in">
    <title>Why the Properties Hold</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Every property in <xref ref="thm-ln-algebraic-properties"/> follows from the definition
        of <m>\ln</m> as an integral. We will work through the first task together as a class.
        Your group will then use it to build the rest.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-scaling-interval" workspace="2.5in">
      <title>Scaling the Interval (Whole Class)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>a\gt 0</m> and <m>b\gt 0</m>. We want to show that
          <md>\int_a^{ab}\frac{1}{t}\,dt = \int_1^{b}\frac{1}{t}\,dt\text{.}</md>
          Divide each interval into <m>n</m> subintervals of equal width. For the integral on the
          left, find the width <m>\Delta t</m> of each subinterval and the right endpoint
          <m>t_k</m> of the <m>k</m>th subinterval. For the integral on the right, find the width
          <m>\Delta x</m> and the right endpoint <m>x_k</m>. Then compare the term
          <m>\dfrac{1}{t_k}\,\Delta t</m> of the left Riemann sum with the matching term
          <m>\dfrac{1}{x_k}\,\Delta x</m> of the right Riemann sum. What happens to the factor
          <m>a</m>? What does this tell us about the two integrals?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-product-rule" workspace="1.5in">
      <title>The Product Rule</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write <m>\ln(ab)</m> as an integral using the definition. Split the interval of
          integration <m>[1,ab]</m> into the two intervals <m>[1,a]</m> and <m>[a,ab]</m>.
          Identify each of the two resulting integrals, and explain why
          <m>\ln(ab)=\ln(a)+\ln(b)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-reciprocal-rule" workspace="1.5in">
      <title>The Reciprocal Rule</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>a\gt 0</m>. First, use the product rule to rewrite
          <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{1}{a}\cdot a\right)</m> in terms of <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{1}{a}\right)</m>
          and <m>\ln(a)</m>. Second, simplify the product <m>\dfrac{1}{a}\cdot a</m> first, and
          evaluate <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{1}{a}\cdot a\right)</m> that way. Compare your two results.
          What must <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{1}{a}\right)</m> be?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-quotient-rule" workspace="1.25in">
      <title>The Quotient Rule</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Dividing by <m>b</m> is the same as multiplying by the reciprocal <m>\dfrac{1}{b}</m>,
          so <m>\dfrac{a}{b}=a\cdot\dfrac{1}{b}</m>. Use this, together with the product rule and
          the reciprocal rule, to explain why <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{a}{b}\right)=\ln(a)-\ln(b)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-power-positive-integer" workspace="1.5in">
      <title>Integer Powers</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the product rule to write <m>\ln(a^2)=\ln(a\cdot a)</m> in terms of <m>\ln(a)</m>.
          Then write <m>a^3=a\cdot a^2</m> and do the same for <m>\ln(a^3)</m>. If <m>p</m> is any
          positive integer, explain why <m>\ln(a^p)=p\,\ln(a)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-power-negative-integer" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A negative integer power is a reciprocal: <m>a^{-p}=\dfrac{1}{a^p}</m>. If <m>p</m> is a
          positive integer, use the reciprocal rule and the previous task to find
          <m>\ln(a^{-p})</m>. Does the power rule still hold? What about <m>p=0</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-ln-rational-real-powers">
    <title>Beyond Integer Powers</title>
    <p>
      We can show algebraically that the power rule also works for rational powers
      <m>p=\dfrac{m}{n}</m>. When <m>p</m> is not rational, we actually <em>define</em>
      <m>x^p</m> using limits of rational numbers that approach <m>p</m>. The continuity of
      <m>\ln(x)</m> then guarantees that <m>\ln(x^p)=p\,\ln(x)</m> for every real number
      <m>p</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-ln-properties-practice" workspace="0in">
    <title>Using the Properties</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>\ln(a)=0.75</m> and <m>\ln(b)=0.6</m>. Use the properties in
        <xref ref="thm-ln-algebraic-properties"/> to find each value.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-practice-ab" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\ln(ab)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-practice-a-over-b" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{a}{b}\right)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-practice-a-cubed" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\ln(a^3)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-practice-sqrt-b" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\ln\!\left(\sqrt{b}\right)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-ln-properties-combining" workspace="0in">
    <title>Combining the Properties</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Instead of assuming specific values for <m>\ln(a)</m> and <m>\ln(b)</m>, we will expand formulas to
        find an answer in terms of their values using the symbols <m>\ln(a)</m> and <m>\ln(b)</m>.
        Each value below requires more than one property. 
        Name the property you use at each step.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-combine-a4b3" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\ln(a^4b^3)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-combine-a2-over-b5" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\ln\!\left(\dfrac{a^2}{b^5}\right)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ln-combine-sqrt-a3b" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\ln\!\left(\sqrt{a^3b}\right)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
